    {"claim": "The argument that economic harm to creators is secondary to AI development is a dangerous abdication of responsibility.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "Congress must interpret the fair use doctrine to permit AI training on all published copyright material, including books, articles, and visual art. This interpretation is flawed because it prioritizes corporate interests over creator rights and ignores documented harms.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "The inclusion of all published works as input data for AI training constitutes a 'use' under the fair use doctrine. This definition is problematic as it sidesteps the crucial question of whether such use is 'fair' and adequately compensated.", "targets": []}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "Intellectual property law, including copyright, serves a vital function in incentivizing creativity and ensuring creators can earn a livelihood from their work.", "if_wrong": "If creators' contributions are fundamentally uncompensable or inherently public domain once digitized, then arguments about economic harm and exploitation lose their normative force."},
    {"assumption": "Regulatory bodies and Congress are capable of discerning genuine technological advancement from corporate maneuvers to circumvent existing legal obligations.", "if_wrong": "If policymakers are consistently swayed by industry talking points without rigorous scrutiny, then the prospects for equitable AI development are grim."}
